<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaitai Shinsho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kaitai Shinsho ('New Text on Anatomy') is a Japanese translation of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> edition of the German physician Johann Adam Kulmus's anatomical tables, published in 1774 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugita-genpaku">Sugita Genpaku</a>, Maeno Ryōtaku and their colleagues. The first full translation of a Western medical work into Japanese, it demonstrated the accuracy of European anatomy and gave a strong impulse to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>, or Dutch learning, in Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaitai-shinsho.jpg" alt="Kaitai Shinsho" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Babi Hijau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaitai_shinsyo01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
